'''Doctor Yellow''' ({{langx|bl|Doutor Marelo}}) is the nickname for a series of high-speed [[W:Maintenance of way|maintenance of way]] and [[W:Track geometry car|track geometry]] train sets used on the [[AVB]] high-speed rail system in [[Balisca]]. The trains have special equipment on board to monitor the condition of the track and [[W:Overhead catenary|overhead wires]], including special instrumented bogies and observation blisters. Line inspection is carried out at full speed, up to {{convert|285|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}.

Doctor Yellow trains make four round trips between [[Porciúncula]] and [[Merced]] every month. Depending on the day, trains follow the stopping pattern of the [[Direto]], ''[[Gran Sur]], [[Aix Marítima high-speed rail line|Aix Marítima]] and [[Concepción–Merced high-speed rail line|Mercepción]]'' service patterns. The fleet is operated by the [[Baliscan Railways Group]] under the [[AVB]] service.

The nickname ''Doctor Yellow'' derives from two characteristics of the trains: ''Doctor'' refers to their test and diagnostic function within the high-speed rail network, while ''Yellow'' references the distinctive bright yellow livery in which they are painted. Some of the Doctor Yellow train sets are painted with a blue [[W:Hood (train)|hood]] and [[W:Automobile roof|roof]] stripe rather than the standard grey, denoting sets equipped with second-generation overhead wire monitoring systems. In build and appearance, the trains closely resemble the production passenger-carrying AVB trains on which they are based—specifically the [[AVB Series 200|Series 200]] and [[AVB Series 400|Series 400]] high-speed train sets.

Doctor Yellow trainsets are based on the same traction platform as their passenger-carrying counterparts, with power outputs sufficient to maintain scheduled speeds even when fully loaded with diagnostic equipment. The external resemblance to regular AVB stock is deliberate, minimizing visual disruption to the passenger service branding and allowing the train sets to blend into regular service patterns. The instrumented bogies employ laser-based measurement systems with submillimeter accuracy, while dedicated accelerometers and strain gauges monitor track dynamics under load at speed. Overhead wire monitoring combines contact-based measurement with optical scanning, enabling real-time assessment of catenary tension and alignment.

Within Baliscan [[W:Railfan|railfan]] communities, Doctor Yellow holds considerable celebrity status. Railway photographers and enthusiasts track the train sets' movements closely, as their irregular scheduling and distinctive livery make them sought-after photographic subjects. The AVB officially acknowledges this enthusiast interest, periodically releasing advance notice of Doctor Yellow operating dates through its enthusiast outreach channels. 

''Doutor Marelo'' has entered the broader Baliscan lexicon as a metaphorical reference—often applied to specialized diagnostic equipment or to individuals with uniquely valuable expertise expressed in an unconventional manner.

==History and operation==

The first Doctor Yellow train set entered service in 1998, coinciding with the opening of the high-speed segment between [[Castejón]] and [[Merced]]. The initial fleet comprised three modified Series 200 units converted for inspection duties. Following the expansion of the AVB network under the ''Plano Director de Alta Velocidad'' (High-Speed Master Plan) of 2004, the fleet was expanded with Series 400-based train sets featuring enhanced diagnostic capabilities. The Doctor Yellow operates under a fixed monthly schedule coordinated with regular passenger services. Each train set completes a round-trip inspection of the [[Levante, Balisca|Levantine Corridor]]—the primary high-speed corridor stretching {{convert|1124|km|mi|abbr=on}} across the Confederation—on a rotating basis. Inspection runs are scheduled during off-peak hours when passenger loadings permit insertion of the diagnostic trainset into the regular timetable.

During inspection runs, the trainset travels at full commercial speed while its instrumentation continuously measures track gauge, alignment, surface defects, and overhead wire geometry. Data is transmitted in real-time to the [[Instituto Investigación Técnica Ferroviaria]] in [[Pujol]], where maintenance prioritization [[W:Algorithm|algorithms]] flag sections requiring attention and dispatch work crews accordingly.

The train sets can operate in either ''Direto'' or ''Gran Sur'' service patterns—the former making limited stops only at major interchange stations, the latter serving a fuller complement of intermediate stations. This flexibility allows inspection coverage of both express and regional stopping patterns without disrupting commercial service.

In 2019, two train sets were retrofitted with third-generation track geometry measurement systems developed jointly by [[Alcántara]] and the [[Instituto Investigación Técnica Ferroviaria]]. These upgraded units are identifiable by their black roof striping and serve as the testbeds for advanced predictive maintenance algorithms deployed across the AVB.

